**Even $n$ fail.** A swim keeps the number of frogs; a jump reduces it by $2$. So the parity of the number of frogs never changes, and from even $n$ we can never reach $1$.

**$n\equiv1,3\pmod8$ work.** Suppose nine consecutive pads, relabelled $1,\dots,9$, are occupied and pad $10$ is empty. The following moves stay inside pads $1$–$10$ and leave only pad $1$ occupied:

1. frog on 7 jumps to 10 (over 8, 9): occupied 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10;
2. frog on 4 jumps to 7 (over 5, 6): occupied 1, 2, 3, 7, 10;
3. frog on 1 swims to 5: occupied 2, 3, 5, 7, 10;
4. frog on 10 swims to 6: occupied 2, 3, 5, 6, 7;
5. frog on 7 jumps to 4 (over 6, 5): occupied 2, 3, 4;
6. frog on 4 jumps to 1 (over 3, 2): occupied 1.

No move lands outside pads $1$–$10$, so frogs further back are never disturbed.

If $n=8k+1$ or $8k+3$ with $k\ge1$, the frogs occupy pads $1,\dots,n$ and pad $n+1$ is empty ($n\le995<1000$). Applying the routine to pads $n-8,\dots,n+1$ leaves frogs on pads $1,\dots,n-8$ with pad $n-7$ empty. Repeating $k$ times leaves $1$ frog (if $n=8k+1$) or frogs on pads $1,2,3$ with pad $4$ empty, and then the frog on $1$ jumps to $4$. So these $n$ work (including $n=1$ and $n=3$).

**$n\equiv5,7\pmod8$ fail.** Split the pads into four classes by label mod $4$; as $4\mid1000$ this is consistent around the circle, and $k\pm4$ is in the same class as $k$. A swim changes no class count. A jump from $k$ to $k\pm3$ over $k\pm1,k\pm2$ involves four pads of four different classes: three classes lose a frog and one gains a frog, so **every jump flips the parity of all four class counts**.

At the end there is one frog, so exactly one class count is odd. After $j$ jumps there are $n-2j$ frogs, so $j=\frac{n-1}2$.
- $n=8k+5$: the counts on pads $1,\dots,n$ are $2k+2,2k+1,2k+1,2k+1$ (classes $1,2,3,0$) — three odd. Reaching exactly one odd class needs an odd number of jumps, but $j=4k+2$ is even.
- $n=8k+7$: the counts are $2k+2,2k+2,2k+2,2k+1$ — one odd. Keeping exactly one odd class needs an even number of jumps, but $j=4k+3$ is odd.

**Answer:** it is possible exactly when $n\equiv1$ or $3\pmod8$ (i.e. $n=1,3,9,11,17,19,\dots,993,995$). ∎

*(A computer search confirms that $5$ and $7$ frogs cannot be reduced to one, and found the six-move routine above.)*
